WEBVTT

00:00:00.709 --> 00:00:05.503 line:78% position:50% align:center
A campus needs cables, a preparation schedule,
and a delivery route.

00:00:05.831 --> 00:00:07.595 line:78% position:50% align:center
These are three different questions.

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:12.774 line:78% position:50% align:center
Before choosing an algorithm, decide what the
answer must describe: an entire

00:00:12.774 --> 00:00:15.747 line:78% position:50% align:center
network, an order, or one path.

00:00:25.042 --> 00:00:27.254 line:78% position:50% align:center
Vertices are numbered from zero.

00:00:27.569 --> 00:00:32.594 line:78% position:50% align:center
A line connects two vertices; an arrow permits
only its direction.

00:00:32.922 --> 00:00:34.483 line:78% position:50% align:center
A weight gives the cost.

00:00:34.811 --> 00:00:40.572 line:78% position:50% align:center
Here, original edge identifier one means the
second input row, even

00:00:40.572 --> 00:00:41.439 line:78% position:50% align:center
after sorting.

00:00:50.598 --> 00:00:55.406 line:78% position:50% align:center
An adjacency list gives each vertex its outgoing
connections.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:01:00.897 line:78% position:50% align:center
An undirected edge needs entries at both ends,
with the same original

00:01:00.897 --> 00:01:01.805 line:78% position:50% align:center
identifier.

00:01:02.133 --> 00:01:07.661 line:78% position:50% align:center
This representation lets us visit neighbours
without scanning the entire input

00:01:07.718 --> 00:01:08.504 line:78% position:50% align:center
each time.

00:01:17.709 --> 00:01:21.416 line:78% position:50% align:center
Disjoint-set union tracks groups as connections
arrive.

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:25.003 line:78% position:50% align:center
Each vertex initially represents itself.

00:01:25.317 --> 00:01:30.723 line:78% position:50% align:center
Parent points to the same vertex, size is one,
and there are five components.

00:01:31.038 --> 00:01:34.717 line:78% position:50% align:center
Only a root stores the authoritative size of its
group.

00:01:36.375 --> 00:01:37.936 line:78% position:50% align:center
Add the link zero to one.

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:41.495 line:78% position:50% align:center
Their roots differ, so two groups merge.

00:01:41.824 --> 00:01:47.231 line:78% position:50% align:center
Attach the smaller tree to the larger one; on
equal sizes, either

00:01:47.231 --> 00:01:48.927 line:78% position:50% align:center
consistent choice works.

00:01:49.283 --> 00:01:51.930 line:78% position:50% align:center
Here, root one joins root zero.

00:01:52.258 --> 00:01:57.827 line:78% position:50% align:center
The number of components drops to four; the
largest group now has two vertices.

00:01:59.459 --> 00:02:01.331 line:78% position:50% align:center
Next join two and three.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:04.171 line:78% position:50% align:center
This creates a second group of size two.

00:02:04.486 --> 00:02:05.761 line:78% position:50% align:center
Now join one and three.

00:02:06.143 --> 00:02:09.117 line:78% position:50% align:center
Find first follows each argument to its root.

00:02:09.445 --> 00:02:13.465 line:78% position:50% align:center
Roots zero and two have size two, so merge them.

00:02:13.834 --> 00:02:18.206 line:78% position:50% align:center
Four vertices share a group; vertex four remains
separate.

00:02:19.778 --> 00:02:23.295 line:78% position:50% align:center
Now add zero to two, then four to itself.

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:26.597 line:78% position:50% align:center
Both operations have equal endpoint roots.

00:02:26.925 --> 00:02:30.347 line:78% position:50% align:center
Do not decrease components or add sizes again.

00:02:30.662 --> 00:02:35.917 line:78% position:50% align:center
Record a state after every operation, including
unchanged states.

00:02:36.219 --> 00:02:40.197 line:78% position:50% align:center
The last two answers are both two components,
largest four.

00:02:41.846 --> 00:02:46.151 line:78% position:50% align:center
Finding vertex three follows three, two, zero.

00:02:46.452 --> 00:02:47.578 line:78% position:50% align:center
Zero is the root.

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.735 line:78% position:50% align:center
Path compression can redirect three straight to
zero.

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:55.513 line:78% position:50% align:center
The group still contains the same four vertices.

00:02:55.841 --> 00:03:01.138 line:78% position:50% align:center
This parent link is internal bookkeeping, not a
new cable in the input graph.

00:03:02.778 --> 00:03:04.991 line:78% position:50% align:center
Return to the state before compression.

00:03:05.319 --> 00:03:07.205 line:78% position:50% align:center
Try one compression step yourself.

00:03:07.534 --> 00:03:09.379 line:78% position:50% align:center
The root is already known.

00:03:09.694 --> 00:03:15.306 line:78% position:50% align:center
Save the current parent, redirect this vertex to
the root, then continue through

00:03:15.306 --> 00:03:16.417 line:78% position:50% align:center
the saved link.

00:03:16.746 --> 00:03:18.428 line:78% position:50% align:center
Pause for up to five minutes.

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:22.205 line:78% position:50% align:center
When ready, compare your three lines with this
fragment.

00:03:23.792 --> 00:03:29.374 line:78% position:50% align:center
Balancing prevents avoidably tall trees, while
compression shortens searches.

00:03:29.689 --> 00:03:31.208 line:78% position:50% align:center
Rank is not group size.

00:03:31.523 --> 00:03:37.094 line:78% position:50% align:center
With both heuristics, the total time for q
operations is order n plus

00:03:37.094 --> 00:03:41.004 line:78% position:50% align:center
q times inverse Ackermann of n, amortized.

00:03:41.333 --> 00:03:43.926 line:78% position:50% align:center
Output storage adds order q.

00:03:44.431 --> 00:03:48.083 line:78% position:50% align:center
Ordinary DSU does not support arbitrary
deletions.

00:03:49.722 --> 00:03:54.435 line:78% position:50% align:center
The highlighted forest connects all five
buildings for total cost ten.

00:03:54.763 --> 00:03:59.720 line:78% position:50% align:center
But delivery from zero to four uses zero, two,
four for cost eight.

00:04:00.021 --> 00:04:02.614 line:78% position:50% align:center
These totals optimize different objects.

00:04:02.929 --> 00:04:06.948 line:78% position:50% align:center
A spanning tree need not preserve every shortest
route.

00:04:08.959 --> 00:04:13.576 line:78% position:50% align:center
If vertex five is isolated, no legal cable can
connect it.

00:04:13.891 --> 00:04:15.234 line:78% position:50% align:center
It is a separate component.

00:04:15.548 --> 00:04:21.282 line:78% position:50% align:center
A spanning forest on n vertices and c components
contains n minus c

00:04:21.282 --> 00:04:22.013 line:78% position:50% align:center
edges.

00:04:22.342 --> 00:04:27.804 line:78% position:50% align:center
A tree also has no redundant links; reliability
requirements need a richer

00:04:27.804 --> 00:04:28.780 line:78% position:50% align:center
network model.

00:04:30.709 --> 00:04:33.587 line:78% position:50% align:center
Kruskal considers edges by increasing weight.

00:04:33.915 --> 00:04:37.133 line:78% position:50% align:center
The original identifiers remain attached to
them.

00:04:37.475 --> 00:04:43.179 line:78% position:50% align:center
Here the examination order is two, one, six,
zero, three,

00:04:43.277 --> 00:04:44.429 line:78% position:50% align:center
five, four.

00:04:44.758 --> 00:04:49.823 line:78% position:50% align:center
Start a fresh disjoint-set structure to
represent the forest being built.

00:04:51.528 --> 00:04:54.787 line:78% position:50% align:center
Edge two joins one and two for cost one.

00:04:55.129 --> 00:04:57.219 line:78% position:50% align:center
Their roots differ, so accept it.

00:04:57.534 --> 00:05:01.417 line:78% position:50% align:center
Edge one then connects zero to that group for
cost two.

00:05:01.705 --> 00:05:07.260 line:78% position:50% align:center
Every accepted edge joins two trees instead of
creating a cycle.

00:05:08.862 --> 00:05:11.957 line:78% position:50% align:center
Edge six joins three and four for cost two.

00:05:12.286 --> 00:05:18.044 line:78% position:50% align:center
We now have two trees: zero, one, two; and
three, four.

00:05:18.373 --> 00:05:21.251 line:78% position:50% align:center
A forest can contain several growing pieces.

00:05:21.566 --> 00:05:26.033 line:78% position:50% align:center
Its running cost is five, and it is still
extendable to an optimum.

00:05:27.612 --> 00:05:30.055 line:78% position:50% align:center
Should edge zero now be accepted?

00:05:30.383 --> 00:05:34.158 line:78% position:50% align:center
Its endpoints already have the path zero, two,
one.

00:05:34.473 --> 00:05:37.446 line:78% position:50% align:center
Adding zero to one would close a cycle.

00:05:37.761 --> 00:05:42.283 line:78% position:50% align:center
Skip it, although its weight is smaller than the
next accepted edge.

00:05:42.612 --> 00:05:45.381 line:78% position:50% align:center
Eligibility matters as well as price.

00:05:47.028 --> 00:05:51.225 line:78% position:50% align:center
Edge three connects the two remaining groups for
cost five.

00:05:51.539 --> 00:05:53.263 line:78% position:50% align:center
Total cost becomes ten.

00:05:53.577 --> 00:05:56.184 line:78% position:50% align:center
The last two edges would create cycles.

00:05:56.499 --> 00:06:01.374 line:78% position:50% align:center
The answer has four original edge identifiers
and one component.

00:06:01.662 --> 00:06:07.450 line:78% position:50% align:center
On a disconnected graph, process every
component, rather than assuming a single

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:08.616 line:78% position:50% align:center
tree exists.

00:06:10.209 --> 00:06:13.073 line:78% position:50% align:center
A cut separates a current component from the
rest.

00:06:13.374 --> 00:06:19.038 line:78% position:50% align:center
A lightest crossing edge is safe for some
optimum containing the current forest.

00:06:19.366 --> 00:06:25.220 line:78% position:50% align:center
If an optimum lacks it, an exchange along the
resulting cycle replaces an edge

00:06:25.236 --> 00:06:26.796 line:78% position:50% align:center
of no smaller weight.

00:06:27.111 --> 00:06:30.057 line:78% position:50% align:center
Equal weights can allow different valid optima.

00:06:31.709 --> 00:06:34.587 line:78% position:50% align:center
Prim solves the same objective with different
state.

00:06:34.902 --> 00:06:36.761 line:78% position:50% align:center
Start at vertex zero.

00:06:37.089 --> 00:06:42.087 line:78% position:50% align:center
Its boundary offers edge zero at cost four and
edge one at cost two.

00:06:42.415 --> 00:06:44.967 line:78% position:50% align:center
Choose edge one to bring in vertex two.

00:06:45.268 --> 00:06:50.810 line:78% position:50% align:center
A heap candidate must retain destination,
weight, and original edge

00:06:50.812 --> 00:06:51.706 line:78% position:50% align:center
identifier.

00:06:54.875 --> 00:06:59.248 line:78% position:50% align:center
Vertex two exposes an edge to one costing one.

00:06:59.576 --> 00:07:02.808 line:78% position:50% align:center
It is now the cheapest boundary edge, so take
it.

00:07:03.136 --> 00:07:05.743 line:78% position:50% align:center
The accepted weights are two, then one.

00:07:06.057 --> 00:07:11.612 line:78% position:50% align:center
Prim is not scanning a globally sorted edge
list; its available choices

00:07:11.642 --> 00:07:13.419 line:78% position:50% align:center
depend on the visited region.

00:07:16.542 --> 00:07:22.208 line:78% position:50% align:center
The old offer from zero to one still exists in a
lazy queue, but one is already

00:07:22.208 --> 00:07:22.858 line:78% position:50% align:center
visited.

00:07:23.172 --> 00:07:25.072 line:78% position:50% align:center
Discard that offer when removed.

00:07:25.387 --> 00:07:30.143 line:78% position:50% align:center
Checking only at insertion is not enough,
because the tree can change while an

00:07:30.143 --> 00:07:31.689 line:78% position:50% align:center
entry waits in the queue.

00:07:34.806 --> 00:07:40.526 line:78% position:50% align:center
The cheapest eligible offer now is edge three,
reaching vertex three for

00:07:40.526 --> 00:07:41.244 line:78% position:50% align:center
five.

00:07:41.572 --> 00:07:43.934 line:78% position:50% align:center
Then edge six reaches four for two.

00:07:44.249 --> 00:07:47.684 line:78% position:50% align:center
Running totals are two, three, eight, ten.

00:07:47.999 --> 00:07:53.243 line:78% position:50% align:center
Kruskal and Prim agree on optimum cost, although
their selection sequences

00:07:53.243 --> 00:07:53.920 line:78% position:50% align:center
differ.

00:07:57.028 --> 00:08:01.619 line:78% position:50% align:center
When the queue is empty, scan for another
unvisited vertex.

00:08:01.933 --> 00:08:07.108 line:78% position:50% align:center
An isolated vertex starts a new component and
contributes no edge.

00:08:07.422 --> 00:08:13.088 line:78% position:50% align:center
A lazy heap may contain order m entries, giving
order n plus m

00:08:13.088 --> 00:08:17.678 line:78% position:50% align:center
log of m plus one time, and order n plus m
memory.

00:08:20.709 --> 00:08:22.840 line:78% position:50% align:center
Now use directed dependencies.

00:08:23.154 --> 00:08:28.372 line:78% position:50% align:center
Depth-first search follows one unfinished
branch, then returns to work it

00:08:28.372 --> 00:08:29.212 line:78% position:50% align:center
suspended.

00:08:29.513 --> 00:08:35.149 line:78% position:50% align:center
A recursive call remembers both the vertex and
where to resume its neighbour list.

00:08:35.464 --> 00:08:38.532 line:78% position:50% align:center
An explicit stack must preserve that progress
too.

00:08:42.612 --> 00:08:46.373 line:78% position:50% align:center
Unvisited means the search has not entered a
vertex.

00:08:46.701 --> 00:08:49.851 line:78% position:50% align:center
Active means it is on the current depth-first
path.

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:54.090 line:78% position:50% align:center
Finished means every outgoing edge has been
examined.

00:08:54.419 --> 00:08:58.615 line:78% position:50% align:center
Here the active stack is zero, one, three, four.

00:08:58.930 --> 00:09:03.058 line:78% position:50% align:center
A visited Boolean alone cannot express these
distinctions.

00:09:07.125 --> 00:09:12.096 line:78% position:50% align:center
Vertex four has no outgoing work, so finish it
and pop its frame.

00:09:12.411 --> 00:09:15.425 line:78% position:50% align:center
Then three finishes, followed by one.

00:09:15.739 --> 00:09:17.707 line:78% position:50% align:center
Zero still has another neighbour.

00:09:18.022 --> 00:09:21.715 line:78% position:50% align:center
Notice that finish records completion, not
discovery.

00:09:22.030 --> 00:09:27.612 line:78% position:50% align:center
This distinction is essential for both Kosaraju
and topological ordering.

00:09:31.625 --> 00:09:32.330 line:78% position:50% align:center
Enter two.

00:09:32.644 --> 00:09:35.835 line:78% position:50% align:center
Its edge reaches three, which has already
finished.

00:09:36.163 --> 00:09:37.982 line:78% position:50% align:center
This is not a directed cycle.

00:09:38.269 --> 00:09:41.202 line:78% position:50% align:center
Continue until two and zero finish.

00:09:41.585 --> 00:09:47.291 line:78% position:50% align:center
For this adjacency order, the finish list is
four, three, one, two,

00:09:47.291 --> 00:09:47.955 line:78% position:50% align:center
zero.

00:09:48.283 --> 00:09:52.194 line:78% position:50% align:center
Other traversal orders may produce different
valid lists.

00:09:56.209 --> 00:10:01.247 line:78% position:50% align:center
At one hundred thousand vertices, relying on a
small call stack can fail.

00:10:01.562 --> 00:10:04.345 line:78% position:50% align:center
Store the next-neighbour index explicitly.

00:10:04.646 --> 00:10:09.997 line:78% position:50% align:center
Advance it before descending, and finish only
when no neighbour remains.

00:10:10.298 --> 00:10:15.978 line:78% position:50% align:center
Every vertex enters and exits once; each edge is
inspected

00:10:15.978 --> 00:10:16.696 line:78% position:50% align:center
once.

00:10:20.695 --> 00:10:25.081 line:78% position:50% align:center
In this graph, zero, one, and two reach one
another.

00:10:25.505 --> 00:10:29.103 line:78% position:50% align:center
Three and four form another mutually reachable
group.

00:10:29.432 --> 00:10:31.739 line:78% position:50% align:center
Vertex five is isolated.

00:10:32.067 --> 00:10:37.176 line:78% position:50% align:center
The arrow from two to three does not merge the
groups, because there is no return

00:10:37.176 --> 00:10:37.853 line:78% position:50% align:center
path.

00:10:40.209 --> 00:10:44.880 line:78% position:50% align:center
Kosaraju first runs depth-first search on the
original graph.

00:10:45.195 --> 00:10:49.527 line:78% position:50% align:center
Cycles are allowed here; finish the pass rather
than rejecting them.

00:10:49.842 --> 00:10:51.728 line:78% position:50% align:center
Use the displayed neighbour order.

00:10:52.111 --> 00:10:56.198 line:78% position:50% align:center
Finishing from zero gives four, three, two, one,
zero.

00:10:56.527 --> 00:10:59.147 line:78% position:50% align:center
Then isolated five finishes.

00:10:59.461 --> 00:11:03.291 line:78% position:50% align:center
Include it even though it was never reachable
from zero.

00:11:05.598 --> 00:11:10.569 line:78% position:50% align:center
Reverse every directed edge, keeping vertex
identities fixed.

00:11:10.897 --> 00:11:14.006 line:78% position:50% align:center
Clear the visited state before the second pass.

00:11:14.335 --> 00:11:18.273 line:78% position:50% align:center
Use decreasing first-pass finish order for the
next seeds.

00:11:18.587 --> 00:11:24.156 line:78% position:50% align:center
The second graph must be the transpose of the
first, rather than a second traversal

00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:25.379 line:78% position:50% align:center
in the same direction.

00:11:27.792 --> 00:11:29.950 line:78% position:50% align:center
Seed five finds only five.

00:11:30.265 --> 00:11:35.290 line:78% position:50% align:center
Seed zero then collects zero, one, and two in
the transpose.

00:11:35.605 --> 00:11:38.157 line:78% position:50% align:center
The later seed two is already assigned.

00:11:38.485 --> 00:11:41.336 line:78% position:50% align:center
Seed three collects three and four.

00:11:41.637 --> 00:11:43.211 line:78% position:50% align:center
Skip assigned vertices.

00:11:43.526 --> 00:11:49.110 line:78% position:50% align:center
Colours name the groups; numeric component
labels need not match this presentation

00:11:49.110 --> 00:11:49.638 line:78% position:50% align:center
order.

00:11:52.056 --> 00:11:55.939 line:78% position:50% align:center
Think of each strongly connected group as one
vertex.

00:11:56.268 --> 00:11:59.322 line:78% position:50% align:center
Their condensation has no directed cycle.

00:11:59.651 --> 00:12:04.950 line:78% position:50% align:center
The remaining group with greatest first-pass
finish time has no outgoing path to

00:12:04.950 --> 00:12:07.705 line:78% position:50% align:center
another unassigned group in the transpose.

00:12:08.034 --> 00:12:11.496 line:78% position:50% align:center
The search stays inside that group and reaches
all of it.

00:12:13.848 --> 00:12:15.979 line:78% position:50% align:center
Return to the original directions.

00:12:16.307 --> 00:12:20.218 line:78% position:50% align:center
Internal edges disappear when groups are
contracted.

00:12:20.533 --> 00:12:23.792 line:78% position:50% align:center
The edge from two to three becomes A to B.

00:12:24.106 --> 00:12:29.745 line:78% position:50% align:center
If we add another original edge from one to
three, it produces the same pair, not a

00:12:29.745 --> 00:12:31.495 line:78% position:50% align:center
second condensation edge.

00:12:31.824 --> 00:12:34.294 line:78% position:50% align:center
The isolated group still exists.

00:12:36.625 --> 00:12:40.781 line:78% position:50% align:center
Mutually dependent modules may need coordinated
changes.

00:12:41.109 --> 00:12:46.720 line:78% position:50% align:center
Condensation exposes dependencies between
groups, but does not fix their internal

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:47.561 line:78% position:50% align:center
cycles.

00:12:47.875 --> 00:12:53.498 line:78% position:50% align:center
Two passes, the transpose, and condensation need
linear work;

00:12:53.935 --> 00:12:58.417 line:78% position:50% align:center
hash-based removal of duplicate pairs gives an
expected linear time bound.

00:13:00.695 --> 00:13:03.614 line:78% position:50% align:center
Return to the acyclic task graph.

00:13:03.915 --> 00:13:07.147 line:78% position:50% align:center
An arrow means its source must happen before its
target.

00:13:07.434 --> 00:13:13.179 line:78% position:50% align:center
Reverse the DFS finish list to obtain zero, two,
one, three, four.

00:13:13.508 --> 00:13:16.236 line:78% position:50% align:center
This order respects every dependency.

00:13:16.551 --> 00:13:22.312 line:78% position:50% align:center
Independent tasks can sometimes exchange places,
so a valid answer need not

00:13:22.312 --> 00:13:23.125 line:78% position:50% align:center
be unique.

00:13:30.042 --> 00:13:33.369 line:78% position:50% align:center
Build each vertex position in the proposed
order.

00:13:33.724 --> 00:13:39.172 line:78% position:50% align:center
For edge one to three, position one is two and
position three is

00:13:39.172 --> 00:13:41.711 line:78% position:50% align:center
three: the inequality holds.

00:13:42.039 --> 00:13:47.311 line:78% position:50% align:center
Check every edge and verify that every vertex
occurs exactly

00:13:47.311 --> 00:13:48.056 line:78% position:50% align:center
once.

00:13:48.371 --> 00:13:53.314 line:78% position:50% align:center
This is useful for build dependencies, but does
not optimize task duration.

00:14:00.278 --> 00:14:02.097 line:78% position:50% align:center
Now add four to zero.

00:14:02.384 --> 00:14:07.602 line:78% position:50% align:center
During the active path zero, one, three, four,
this edge

00:14:07.602 --> 00:14:09.339 line:78% position:50% align:center
returns to active zero.

00:14:09.653 --> 00:14:12.477 line:78% position:50% align:center
No complete order can respect that cycle.

00:14:12.792 --> 00:14:15.806 line:78% position:50% align:center
Return acyclic false and an empty order.

00:14:16.134 --> 00:14:21.460 line:78% position:50% align:center
A cycle anywhere in the graph, including a
disconnected part, makes a complete

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:23.822 line:78% position:50% align:center
topological order impossible.

00:14:30.682 --> 00:14:34.076 line:78% position:50% align:center
A workpiece can take alternative processing
routes.

00:14:34.404 --> 00:14:39.907 line:78% position:50% align:center
Vertices are stages reached; arrows are allowed
next transitions; weights are

00:14:39.907 --> 00:14:40.774 line:78% position:50% align:center
costs.

00:14:41.103 --> 00:14:43.763 line:78% position:50% align:center
We seek the cheapest route from source zero.

00:14:44.092 --> 00:14:49.456 line:78% position:50% align:center
These alternatives are not a list of mandatory
tasks that must all be performed.

00:14:51.542 --> 00:14:54.366 line:78% position:50% align:center
Only the source cost is known initially.

00:14:54.681 --> 00:14:57.355 line:78% position:50% align:center
Null means no route has yet been discovered.

00:14:57.670 --> 00:15:03.496 line:78% position:50% align:center
Process vertices in a topological order and
never relax from a null distance.

00:15:03.825 --> 00:15:09.490 line:78% position:50% align:center
After processing zero, costs to one and two are
five and two, with original

00:15:09.490 --> 00:15:11.173 line:78% position:50% align:center
parent edges zero and one.

00:15:13.306 --> 00:15:18.684 line:78% position:50% align:center
Without a discount, processing one first offers
seven to vertex three.

00:15:19.012 --> 00:15:23.616 line:78% position:50% align:center
Processing two improves that to six and offers
eleven to four.

00:15:23.931 --> 00:15:26.333 line:78% position:50% align:center
Then three improves four to nine.

00:15:26.648 --> 00:15:32.368 line:78% position:50% align:center
The route zero, two, three, four costs two plus
four plus three, which is

00:15:32.368 --> 00:15:32.882 line:78% position:50% align:center
nine.

00:15:35.042 --> 00:15:40.624 line:78% position:50% align:center
Now change only edge one to three to minus four,
representing a discount,

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:42.621 line:78% position:50% align:center
and restart the calculation.

00:15:42.950 --> 00:15:45.814 line:78% position:50% align:center
Negative weights are valid in a DAG.

00:15:46.143 --> 00:15:50.665 line:78% position:50% align:center
After zero is processed again, the cost to three
is still null.

00:15:50.980 --> 00:15:54.591 line:78% position:50% align:center
We have not processed its incoming alternatives
yet.

00:15:56.612 --> 00:16:01.759 line:78% position:50% align:center
At vertex one, the candidate is five plus minus
four, giving one.

00:16:02.114 --> 00:16:07.547 line:78% position:50% align:center
Three has no known cost, so store one and
original edge identifier two.

00:16:07.889 --> 00:16:11.514 line:78% position:50% align:center
The parent is an edge identifier, not vertex
one.

00:16:11.816 --> 00:16:16.528 line:78% position:50% align:center
Updating the number without its witness would
leave an inconsistent result.

00:16:18.625 --> 00:16:21.938 line:78% position:50% align:center
Vertex two later offers six to three.

00:16:22.267 --> 00:16:27.427 line:78% position:50% align:center
Six is worse than one, so retain the existing
distance and parent.

00:16:27.742 --> 00:16:29.669 line:78% position:50% align:center
It also offers eleven to four.

00:16:29.998 --> 00:16:34.425 line:78% position:50% align:center
Vertex three then offers four to vertex four.

00:16:34.753 --> 00:16:40.460 line:78% position:50% align:center
The final route is zero, one, three, four, with
total five minus

00:16:40.460 --> 00:16:42.821 line:78% position:50% align:center
four plus three, equal to four.

00:16:44.862 --> 00:16:50.446 line:78% position:50% align:center
An added isolated vertex five finishes with null
distance and parent

00:16:50.446 --> 00:16:51.259 line:78% position:50% align:center
minus one.

00:16:51.560 --> 00:16:54.588 line:78% position:50% align:center
A finite distance can itself be minus one.

00:16:54.916 --> 00:17:00.310 line:78% position:50% align:center
Also reject a directed cycle anywhere in the
input, even if source zero

00:17:00.310 --> 00:17:01.408 line:78% position:50% align:center
cannot reach it.

00:17:01.750 --> 00:17:04.683 line:78% position:50% align:center
That is a whole-graph requirement of this
assignment.

00:17:06.695 --> 00:17:12.046 line:78% position:50% align:center
To reconstruct the route to four, its parent
edge is identifier four:

00:17:12.374 --> 00:17:13.568 line:78% position:50% align:center
three to four.

00:17:13.869 --> 00:17:14.750 line:78% position:50% align:center
Move to three.

00:17:15.078 --> 00:17:17.562 line:78% position:50% align:center
Parent edge two leads from one to three.

00:17:17.864 --> 00:17:20.905 line:78% position:50% align:center
Parent edge zero leads from zero to one.

00:17:21.220 --> 00:17:27.005 line:78% position:50% align:center
Reverse the collected vertices to obtain zero,
one, three, four.

00:17:29.028 --> 00:17:30.860 line:78% position:50% align:center
Try the backward step yourself.

00:17:31.175 --> 00:17:35.684 line:78% position:50% align:center
Original edge four is the row three, four,
three.

00:17:35.999 --> 00:17:38.537 line:78% position:50% align:center
Which column gives the previous vertex?

00:17:38.865 --> 00:17:39.746 line:78% position:50% align:center
Pause if needed.

00:17:40.075 --> 00:17:42.966 line:78% position:50% align:center
It is column zero: vertex three.

00:17:43.295 --> 00:17:47.613 line:78% position:50% align:center
Column two gives the last edge weight, not the
total route cost.

00:17:49.695 --> 00:17:51.649 line:78% position:50% align:center
A correct sum is not enough.

00:17:51.978 --> 00:17:56.622 line:78% position:50% align:center
Check forest identifiers, connectivity, and edge
count.

00:17:56.964 --> 00:18:02.424 line:78% position:50% align:center
For components, verify mutual reachability and
deduplicated condensation.

00:18:02.820 --> 00:18:05.549 line:78% position:50% align:center
For an order, check every edge position.

00:18:05.918 --> 00:18:09.652 line:78% position:50% align:center
For routes, verify parent direction and total
weight.

00:18:09.967 --> 00:18:12.926 line:78% position:50% align:center
Alternative valid witnesses should be accepted.

00:18:14.862 --> 00:18:20.050 line:78% position:50% align:center
Include empty graphs where allowed, isolates,
repeated unions,

00:18:20.174 --> 00:18:25.351 line:78% position:50% align:center
loops, parallel edges, negative weights, and
totals beyond the signed

00:18:25.351 --> 00:18:26.802 line:78% position:50% align:center
thirty-two-bit range.

00:18:27.117 --> 00:18:30.783 line:78% position:50% align:center
Test deep chains and mixed requests in one
process.

00:18:31.098 --> 00:18:36.286 line:78% position:50% align:center
Benchmark Kruskal and Prim on the same graphs at
three sizes.

00:18:36.614 --> 00:18:41.721 line:78% position:50% align:center
At each size, run each method at least three
times and report its median.

00:18:42.036 --> 00:18:44.615 line:78% position:50% align:center
Exclude compilation and document warm-up.

00:18:46.472 --> 00:18:51.918 line:78% position:50% align:center
Here n counts vertices, m input edges, and q
unions.

00:18:52.247 --> 00:18:55.587 line:78% position:50% align:center
DSU is amortized almost linear.

00:18:55.915 --> 00:19:00.138 line:78% position:50% align:center
Kruskal and lazy Prim include sorting or heap
work.

00:19:00.453 --> 00:19:06.062 line:78% position:50% align:center
Kosaraju, topological ordering, and DAG
relaxation are linear;

00:19:06.567 --> 00:19:09.337 line:78% position:50% align:center
hash deduplication is expected linear.

00:19:09.638 --> 00:19:13.807 line:78% position:50% align:center
A reconstructed route takes time proportional to
its length.

00:19:15.709 --> 00:19:17.079 line:78% position:50% align:center
Use the graphs template.

00:19:17.393 --> 00:19:20.638 line:78% position:50% align:center
Its adapters handle JSON input and output.

00:19:20.953 --> 00:19:26.168 line:78% position:50% align:center
Write the six required algorithms and your route
helper in your chosen language.

00:19:26.483 --> 00:19:32.067 line:78% position:50% align:center
Keep original edge identifiers, reset state per
request, use wide

00:19:32.067 --> 00:19:36.318 line:78% position:50% align:center
totals, and send only result JSON to standard
output.

00:19:38.125 --> 00:19:40.868 line:78% position:50% align:center
Save your tests and English analysis.

00:19:41.196 --> 00:19:46.685 line:78% position:50% align:center
Public checks report pass or fail; they do not
calculate the practical grade or

00:19:46.685 --> 00:19:48.273 line:78% position:50% align:center
transfer marks to Moodle.

00:19:48.601 --> 00:19:50.691 line:78% position:50% align:center
Submit the exact commit link.

00:19:50.979 --> 00:19:53.082 line:78% position:50% align:center
An Actions link may support it.

00:19:53.451 --> 00:19:58.911 line:78% position:50% align:center
Follow the current README and Moodle for the
rubric, dates, and submission details.
